// Practice quiz: anti-joins and self-joins
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters and treats colon, equals, hash, tilde and braces as syntax, so those are escaped.

$CATEGORY: sql-jdbc-foundations/crud-aggregates-and-joins/anti-joins-and-self-joins

::o1 customers with no orders::Which query shape finds customers who have no orders? {
=customers LEFT JOIN orders on the customer key, keeping rows where orders.order_id IS NULL#Correct. Unmatched customers get NULL in every orders column, and the order key is never NULL in a real order.
~customers LEFT JOIN orders, keeping rows where orders.order_id IS NOT NULL#Not quite. That keeps the customers who do have orders.
~customers INNER JOIN orders, keeping rows where orders.order_id IS NULL#Not quite. An inner join has already dropped the unmatched customers, so nothing is left.
~customers INNER JOIN orders, keeping rows where orders.total \= 0#Not quite. A zero-total order is still an order.
}

::o1 NOT EXISTS form::Which NOT EXISTS subquery finds customers with no orders? {
=One that selects from orders where the order points at the outer customer#Correct. NOT EXISTS is true for a customer when that customer-specific subquery returns no row.
~One that selects from orders with no link to the outer customer#Not quite. If any order exists at all, it is true for nobody.
~One that selects the customer itself from customers#Not quite. The customer row always exists, so NOT EXISTS is never true.
~One that counts orders and returns the count#Not quite. A count row always exists, even when the count is 0.
}

::o2 self-join condition::employees(emp_id, name, manager_id)\: manager_id points at another employee's emp_id. Which join pairs each employee e with their manager m? {
=employees AS e JOIN employees AS m ON e.manager_id \= m.emp_id#Correct. The employee row supplies manager_id, and the manager row is the one whose emp_id matches it.
~employees AS e JOIN employees AS m ON e.emp_id \= m.emp_id#Not quite. That pairs every employee with themselves.
~employees AS e JOIN employees AS m ON e.manager_id \= m.manager_id#Not quite. That pairs colleagues who share a manager.
~employees JOIN employees ON manager_id \= emp_id#Not quite. PostgreSQL rejects it\: the same table name appears twice in FROM, so each occurrence needs its own alias.
}

::o2 why two aliases::Why does a self-join give the same table two aliases? {
=So each occurrence can be named in its own role, such as employee and manager#Correct. The aliases make each copy a distinct source that columns can be qualified with.
~To make PostgreSQL copy the table first#Not quite. An alias names a reference; nothing is copied.
~Because a table may only be joined once#Not quite. A table can appear several times, but each appearance needs its own name.
~To sort the result#Not quite. Aliases do not sort anything.
}

::o3 NOT IN with a NULL::SELECT name FROM authors WHERE author_id NOT IN (SELECT author_id FROM books); and one book has author_id NULL. What does the query return? {
=No rows#Correct. With a NULL in the list, NOT IN is never true for any author, so WHERE keeps nothing.
~Authors who have no books#Not quite. That is the intent, but the NULL makes every NOT IN test unknown or false.
~Every author#Not quite. Authors with books are excluded, and the rest become unknown.
~An ERROR#Not quite. The query is legal; it just selects nothing.
}

::o3 safe replacement::Which rewrite finds authors with no books even when books.author_id can be NULL? {
=NOT EXISTS with a subquery matching books to the outer author#Correct. NOT EXISTS only asks whether a matching row exists, so NULL keys in other rows do not matter.
~NOT IN with the same subquery and DISTINCT#Not quite. DISTINCT keeps the NULL in the list.
~IN instead of NOT IN#Not quite. IN finds the authors who do have books.
~NOT IN with ORDER BY in the subquery#Not quite. Sorting the list does not remove its NULL.
}
